\f1\fs22 \'91Can I help you?\'92 she challenged the man in Breezy Brains\'92 office.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The man looked at Sarah briefly, then resumed his sleazing around the room. Sarah decided it was sleazing, because he was a heavy-breathing, square-headed character. He had eyebrows that reminded her of caterpillars, and teeth that spoke of drug addiction. Questionably tall and suspiciously fidgety, he went about the office touchy-feeling shelves, opening cupboards, sliding out drawers and examining the undersides of coffee table figurines.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah held her breath while convincing herself it was not from fear. \'91Well?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91I am to sign some documents,\'92 he said, and scratched into his black hair.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Are you expecting to find them under the office art? How about the picture on the wall?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The man smiled like a snake. \'91Ah, yes. Very good,\'92 he said, wagging a crooked finger. He sleazed the painting from the wall, looked at its back, and ran his fingers around the frame. Disappointed, he set the artwork down. \'91I will see the files now.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>If Sarah\'92s blood was chill before, it became frozen margarita slushing through her veins. She looked about the wider office, searching for security or a brave man (
\f2\i Ha! A brave man!
\f1\i0 ) but saw only colleagues gawking over partitions with startled eyes and mouths that formed vowels of horror.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91What kind of documents?\'92 she asked.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91The kind you have, and I want,\'92 he said, and Sarah knew she was being threatened.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Mr. Teh has retired.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Oh?\'92 This information alarmed him, to the extent he stopped prowling the office and bore his full attention on Sarah.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91It will take some time to put his outstanding matters in order,\'92 she answered. \'91Perhaps, if you make an appointment\'97\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91I will see the files now. Please.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah winced as he advanced on her and pulled a particle pistol from his jacket. She screamed, which stopped him cold. She blinked; he furrowed his brow and cocked his head, as if that would loosen the reason he hadn\'92t killed her yet. As he became more confused, Sarah became more lucid.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91It\'92s clear you\'92re not looking for documents,\'92 she said to the suddenly placid giant. \'91Who are you?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The question reacquainted him with his train of thought, but it seemed to Sarah, by the hardened look in his eyes, his train was on a track of an entirely different gauge. He scowled and blinked, looked to the floor, lifted his foot and saw his dark shoe-print on the still-moist, freshly steamed carpet. He sniffed the air, and smelled death and cleaning fluids.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Mr Teh retired here, in this office,\'92 he said.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sensing she had the measure of him now, Sarah said, \'91It\'92s not policy to divulge\'97\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91There was a cleaner.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Yes,\'92 Sarah conceded, before she\'92d thought much of it.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91He left recently.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Yes. You didn\'92t see him on his way out?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The man\'92s face hardened and his jaw chewed, as if that was the way he processed information. \'91The cleaner has the documents,\'92 he concluded.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91No, I doubt\'97\'91\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91The cleaner has the documents,\'92 he repeated, and pushed Sarah aside easier than a cardboard cut-out. He marched for reception, and took a lift to the building\'92s secure hover-pad floor where his stealth copt was waiting. As soon as he was safe in the starboard couch, it\'92s military-grade over-engineered fans revved up and carried him away.\

